    {id: "cc [...args]", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "525e5a509dadb0e9", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "help --json", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "46c8832d2b33787c", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "idioms", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "d4c9704dea64ad73", reviewer: "reader-zi-cdi-picker-exception", author: "implementer-zi-cdi-picker-exception", date: "2026-08-25", note: "`why` text untouched this session (still `Every one of these is a failure this environment has actually seen: reaching for a tool that is not installed, or piping a table into a line-oriented filter and getting nothing.`); re-read because the digest keys on the `use`/`why` pair and `use` changed under R2 of zi-cdi-picker-exception spec01 (`zi` -> `zi`/`cdi` as the fzf exception). Stands: the `use` states the four rules, the `why` states their provenance, which the `use` nowhere says — the pair still does not restate each other with the pair named instead of `zi` alone. Re-digested why-digest(new use, unchanged why) and it reproduces the spec's precomputed d4c9704dea64ad73; the prior digest 27c66c667c57cd84 was independently reproduced against the old `use` text first. Carried forward, not resolved by this edit: the pre-existing watch item from the prior review — the `why`'s example failure `reaching for a tool that is not installed` sits beside a `use` clause saying fzf IS installed and is what `zi`/`cdi` open, reading as a generalisation over the four rules rather than a claim about fzf, so not false but the clause to sharpen if the `why` is ever reopened."}
    {id: "help", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "29ed1a89c9b22b7c", reviewer: "reader-H-2", author: "impl-H-2", date: "2026-08-23", note: "Read for restatement, then checked the settled claim. New in `why` and absent from `use`: the collision case, that ours wins, the `std/help` tail on a `command` entry, and the three flags — the `use` names none of them. Sentence 1 overlaps the `use`'s std/help clause in wording but adds the constraint the `use` omits: nushell's sanctioned override routes every `<x> --help` here involuntarily, so delegation is a duty, not a courtesy. Verified against help.nu: clause 7 resolves a documented name to the manual, `_help_entry_detail` appends `core-help` (= `std/help`) for a `command` verify target, and clauses 1-3 are `--delegate`/`--entry`/`--topic`. Matches PRD R4 and R10."}
    {id: "help --check", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "1a29cf82f30a8d3e", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21", note: "borderline and left standing: the why's second sentence re-walks the use's report list before ranking which of the three failures is dangerous. The ranking is the payload"}
    {id: "pass <tab>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "a359d049a9d14a90", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "theme", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "ae0e20b8b0a918f5", reviewer: "reader-S-9", author: "impl-S-9", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` says what happens (retint on browse, apply on Enter, nothing on Esc); the `why` says why it is built that way — browsing never runs `tinty apply`, the preview is one OSC 11 because an apply per focus fires the whole hook chain, and the one real apply runs after tv exits so the hooks see the real env. None of that is in the `use`, and all of it is verified against theme.nu's own header contract and the preview script. No restatement."}
    {id: "theme toggle", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "e7d26408ce324641", reviewer: "reader-S-9", author: "impl-S-9", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` describes the flip; the `why` carries the design decision the `use` never mentions — A/B not light/dark, a slot holds whatever was last picked while active, the picker retunes the active slot rather than choosing one. All three clauses check against theme.nu:23-26 and `_theme_commit`'s contract comment, and the A/B-not-light/dark point is spec01's explicit R2 correction. New information, not restatement."}

    # nvim.nuon
    {id: "<leader>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "71e4b47556eb7e04", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<Esc>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "6f287024a7a967f1", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r7", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "both fields revised this session; the `why`'s old opening re-said the `use`, and a previous reader called it a restatement, so it was cut. Stands as rewritten: the `use` says the key is inert today, the `why` says why that is a decision rather than an accident — option and map are one decision, live bug L-6 RESOLVED it as `port hlsearch = false, drop the map` (docs/capabilities-nvim.md:46, whose rejected alternative is exactly the `other way round` the why names), so this documents a binding the rebuild will not carry. Live state matches: keymaps.lua:4 binds it, options.lua:30 sets hlsearch = false. Recorded for whoever owns coverage, not a defect here: an entry documenting a map slated for removal while carrying a live `nvim-map` verify target is a content-model question."}
    {id: "<C-d> <C-u> n N", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "a5ec320811e78193", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>p (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "a5ec5256b6d11b2b", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<S-Up> <S-Down> <S-Left> <S-Right>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "f26cd1d68c5903ce", reviewer: "cc-1787432671", author: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21", note: "re-read by a session that wrote neither field. Stands: the use now states only the observable oddity — from insert, <S-Left> catches two characters — and the why states the cause, that the insert cursor sits between characters and leaving insert drops it back. The why's closing `the extra character is the price` names that observation once more, but as the conclusion of the explanation, which is what an explanation is for"}
    {id: "h j k l (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "621bc37825b63168", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
