    {id: "<Tab> <S-Tab> <C-n> <C-p> <C-Space> <C-e>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "8ba5e6770c0e113c", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<BS> and <CR> (autopairs)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "79d3149fc3ef4c30", reviewer: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps-r1", author: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps", date: "2026-08-25", note: "the `use` states the two observable gestures only (BS deletes a pair as one press, CR splits the bracket unless a menu is open); the `why` supplies the mechanism the `use` never names — blink.cmp's <CR> keymap is {\"accept\", \"fallback\"} (completion.lua:15), fallback runs whatever <CR> mapping existed before blink.cmp claimed the key, and because autopairs.lua sets up before completion.lua in load order that prior mapping is autopairs' own bracket-expand handler, so the expand survives only through the fallback chain and only while no menu is open; <BS> has no such chain, it is the one map nothing else claims. No restatement: the use never mentions blink.cmp, load order or fallback. Measured headless against this repo's own config, with InsertEnter allowed to settle for blink.cmp's async keymap setup: filetype lua, type `local t = {` then <CR> with no menu open -> buffer becomes ['local t = {', '  ', '}'] (bracket still expands), and maparg confirms <BS> -> 'autopairs delete' (buffer=1), <CR> -> 'blink.cmp: Accept' (buffer=1) — matching autopairs.lua's own header comment read correctly (its plain claim that map_cr 'does not survive' is about which desc string wins, not about the behaviour disappearing)."}
    {id: "<leader>e", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "3d35cc67da8009d6", reviewer: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps-r1", author: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps", date: "2026-08-25", note: "the old `why` said oil was lazy on <leader>e and that `:e some/dir` reached neither oil nor netrw before that press — backwards. Confirmed `lazy = false` at home/dot_config/nvim/lua/plugins/explorer.lua:38, and measured headless against this repo's own config (XDG_CONFIG_HOME at home/dot_config): `:e <cwd>` with no key pressed gives filetype=oil, bufname=oil:///Users/feb/dev/dotfiles/. So oil's default_file_explorer hijack is live at startup and :e reaches it with no key press; netrw stays disabled either way. The `use` is unchanged and still describes <leader>e correctly as a working fast path."}
    {id: "<leader>cf", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "7755bc6ab92859da", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>t", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "e70cd08965d13ba7", reviewer: "reader-leader-t-why", author: "implementer-markdown-tables", date: "2026-08-23", note: "the `use` asserts only that alignment is already on in a markdown buffer; the `why` supplies the mechanism it never names, and both halves check out. The plugin really does ship inactive and per buffer — `table_mode_always_active` defaults to 0 (plugin/table-mode.vim:33) and `tablemode#IsActive` reads the buffer-local `b:table_mode_active` (autoload/tablemode.vim:146, 192-195) — so the FileType autocmd in table-mode.lua is what switches it on, and the counterfactual is measured, not asserted: tests/nvim-markdown-tables.sh cf2 deletes the autocmd and gets `b2_active=nil` on `:edit t2.md` while the first buffer stays `active=1`. This replaces the false reason the previous row (impl-H-1-r7, digest 3990fd6e3dddb2d7) vouched for after reading the live config's comment rather than measuring it, so the correction is real and the digest move is earned. One imprecision recorded rather than fixed, since a reader may not edit the text: `the file you opened nvim with` is exact for the fixture this was measured on (`nvim t.md`), but what the redundant direct `enable()` covers is whichever buffer was current at plugin load — start bare and `:e a.md` and that first `:edit` aligns too, so the counterfactual names a case, not the rule. Also noted as thin rather than wrong: nothing here carries the reason most specific to this entry's own key, that the `<leader>t` prefix is set in `init` because plugin/table-mode.vim:48-58 derives nine map names from it at load time, and that the value buys no behaviour since it is already the plugin default."}
    {id: "<leader>ff and <leader><space>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "078b4f8e3e835629", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<Tab> <S-Tab> <CR> (telescope)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "88bce566500afd15", reviewer: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps-r1", author: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps", date: "2026-08-25", note: "the `why`'s old claim 'there is no lhs to introspect' is refuted, not merely superseded: measured headless against this repo's own config (XDG_CONFIG_HOME at home/dot_config), with a live `Telescope find_files` picker open, `vim.fn.maparg('<Tab>', 'i', false, true)` returns a full table with `lhs = '<Tab>'` and `buffer = 1` — an lhs plainly is there to introspect. `kind: prose` still stands, on the real constraint: telescope.lua:41-58 sets these three keys inside `telescope.setup({ defaults = { mappings = ... } })`, which telescope applies as a buffer-local map on the prompt buffer it creates when a picker opens, not at config-load time and not on any buffer before or after. So maparg sees the map only from inside a live picker on that picker's own buffer; no static/headless-with-no-picker probe has anything to check against. The `use` is unchanged and still matches (typing-or-normal-mode, worse/better direction)."}
    {id: "gd and gI", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "f6b0881d074e0d31", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Neovim's own LSP keys", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "8a795c015b386192", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r7", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the `use` was rewritten this session (]d/[d open no float on 0.12; <C-w>d is the key that does); the `why` was not. Stands: the `use` lists the keys, the `why` answers the question it raises but cannot address — why keys this config does not own appear in a manual of this config, and that they are the single stated exception to the allowlist rule. Both checked: ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/lsp.lua:21-33 adds only gd, gI, <leader>rn, <leader>ca and re-maps none of grn/gra/grr/gri/gO/K/]d/[d; the exception is real at prds/06-help/04-drift-check/prd.md:42-44. One imprecision left standing, below the refusal line: `from 0.11 onward` is right for grn/gra/grr/gri/gO, while K, ]d, [d and <C-w>d were defaults from 0.10 — under-dated rather than false."}

    {id: "<leader>ss <leader>sl <leader>sd", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "a3d233b599fb26ea", reviewer: "reader-07-06-nvim-session", author: "analyst-07-06-nvim-session", date: "2026-08-29", note: "independent reading by a session that wrote none of this text. keymaps.lua line 47 confirms leader-q is a plain leaf map, cmd quit, desc Quit, so the timeoutlen-prefix reasoning holds. tmux-resurrect's nvim_session.sh does take the pane DIRECTORY and echo a restore command (nvim -S when a Session.vim exists, else the original command), so re-launches nvim in the pane's directory is accurate as a general description of the strategy family, though this literal script is the Session.vim strategy rather than the custom-command inline strategy session.lua's own comments say this config actually uses. The why adds new information not in use: the reboot/tmux-resurrect motivation and the leader-q-collision rationale for the keybinding choice, no restatement. Word count: 69."}
